From etna-haskell-pipeline Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Subject: [PATCH] integer_instance_word_overflow_1d65a86_1

Synthetic patch reflecting upstream commit 1d65a86 ("fixed Integer
instance"). Reverse-applies (`git apply -R`) to install the original
bug; forward-applies to restore the fix on the base tree.

Bug summary: the buggy Integer instance maps Integer through Word via
`fromIntegral`. For negative Integers, `fromIntegral :: Integer -> Word`
performs modular wrap-around (e.g. `fromIntegral (-1 :: Integer) :: Word`
== `maxBound :: Word`), so all negative inputs collapse onto large
positive Word indices. The trie itself stores
`f . fromIntegral . toInteger`, which then applies `f` to that very
large positive integer rather than to the original negative input. The
end result: `untrie (trie f) n /= f n` for every negative n.

The fixed (current HEAD) instance encodes the value as `(sign, bits abs)`
via `bitsZ`/`unbitsZ`, which is total over Integer.

The unused helpers `unbitsZ`/`bitsZ` are intentionally left in place
when the bug is installed to keep the diff minimal; they trip an
unused-binding warning under -Wall but neither the upstream library nor
the runner uses -Werror.
---
 src/Data/MemoTrie.hs | 8 ++++----
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/Data/MemoTrie.hs b/src/Data/MemoTrie.hs
--- a/src/Data/MemoTrie.hs
+++ b/src/Data/MemoTrie.hs
@@ -496,10 +496,10 @@ IntInstance(Int64,Word64,Int64Trie)
 -- extract the sign bit.

 instance HasTrie Integer where
-  newtype Integer :->: a = IntegerTrie (Word :->: a)
-  trie f = IntegerTrie (trie (f . fromIntegral . toInteger))
-  untrie (IntegerTrie t) n = untrie t (fromIntegral n)
-  enumerate (IntegerTrie t) = enum' fromIntegral t
+  newtype Integer :->: a = IntegerTrie ((Bool,[Bool]) :->: a)
+  trie f = IntegerTrie (trie (f . unbitsZ))
+  untrie (IntegerTrie t) = untrie t . bitsZ
+  enumerate (IntegerTrie t) = enum' unbitsZ t


 unbitsZ :: (Num n, Bits n) => (Bool,[Bool]) -> n
